# Line endings, decided by the repository rather than by each machine.
#
# Without this, `core.autocrlf` answers the question, and it is set per installation: the same
# clone comes out with different bytes depending on who made it. Git said as much on `git add`,
# warning that it would rewrite `gradlew` with CRLF the next time it touched the file.
#
# Everything is normalised to LF in the repository. What differs is what lands in the working
# copy, and only where the format demands it.

* text=auto

# Must stay LF, wherever they are checked out.
#
# A shell script read with CRLF fails on its own first line: the interpreter looks for `/bin/sh\r`
# and does not find it. `gradlew` has no extension, so it is named outright.
*.sh            text eol=lf
gradlew         text eol=lf

# Must be CRLF, for the opposite reason: the Windows command interpreter mis-parses LF-only batch
# files, silently and only sometimes.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf

# Binary: never normalised, never diffed as text.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.webp          binary
*.avif          binary
*.ico           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.wasm          binary
*.glb           binary
*.gltf          binary
*.riv           binary
*.jar           binary
*.keystore      binary
*.apk           binary
*.aab           binary
*.af            binary
